Understanding Project Structure as a DevOps Engineer

When applying for DevOps roles, job descriptions often mention Java, Node.js, or other technologies. This doesn’t mean you are expected to develop code, but you should understand how projects are structured, how frontend, backend, and databases interact, and how to deploy and manage them efficiently.

Here’s a simplified example using a Task Management App:


---

1. Project Layers

1. Frontend (User Interface)

Built using React, Angular, or any web technology.

Handles what the user sees and interacts with.



2. Backend (API / Application Server)

Node.js, Java Spring Boot, or other frameworks.

Processes user requests, interacts with the database, and returns results.



3. Database

MySQL, PostgreSQL, or MongoDB.

Stores and retrieves application data.





---

2. Sample Project Structure

task-manager/
├── backend/               # Backend server
│   ├── app.js             # Main server file
│   ├── routes/            # API routes
│   ├── controllers/       # Business logic
│   └── db/                # Database configuration
├── frontend/              # Frontend application
│   ├── src/
│   │   ├── App.js
│   │   └── components/
│   └── package.json
└── README.md

> As a DevOps engineer, you’ll see this structure to configure builds, deployments, and pipelines, not necessarily write the code.




---

3. How Frontend, Backend, and Database Connect

[React Frontend] <--HTTP API--> [Node.js / Java Backend] <--SQL--> [MySQL Database]

1. Frontend sends HTTP requests to the backend.


2. Backend handles the requests and queries the database.


3. Database stores or retrieves data and sends it back to the backend.


4. Backend returns the response to the frontend.




---

4. Why This Matters for DevOps

Deployment: Knowing project layers helps decide where to deploy (WebLogic, Tomcat, Nginx, Kubernetes).

CI/CD: Helps in creating pipelines for frontend, backend, and database changes.

Monitoring & Logging: You know what to monitor in each layer (server logs, DB performance, API response times).

Containerization: You can dockerize each layer independently.



---

5. Example Real-World Setup

Layer	Technology	DevOps Role Example

Frontend	React.js	Build & deploy to Nginx or cloud storage
Backend	Node.js / Java	Dockerize, deploy on Tomcat / WebLogic / Kubernetes
Database	MySQL / MongoDB	Setup backups, monitoring, replication
